{
  "version": 343,
  "released": "2026-09-24",
  "changelog": "v343: a card is asked only when a person could answer it. WHERE IT WAS SEEN: the owner's staging review of 2026-09-24 found four live cards with no quality bar under them. “When was they?” — node `node:63099d24ba8c9fe2e6dd800a`, from `claim:65573521952535272873944a`: a `general_listener` claim over his one-line mission-card answer (“19-21 years old”), `subject_mention: \"they\"`, `event_kind: span`, `confidence: 0.0`, no `event_mention` — minted a node and a precision-gap card and should never have minted either. “When did Harvey arriving happen?” — a bare gerund phrase (“Harvey arriving”) dropped verbatim into the fallback `\"When did {what} happen?\"` template. “Which James or Anthon James Taylor is 'James' here?” and “Which Dave or Kids is 'Dave' here?” — the roster held a `maps_to_focus` alias row (`james` -> `anthon-james-taylor`) riding along as its own candidate for a name already carried as an alias on the canonical row, and the collective row `Kids` rode along on the owner's own first name via an alias (`Dave's kids`) that merely starts with it — while the genuinely distinct second James, the owner's son James Everett Taylor, was never even offered. WHAT BROKE: the question-candidate flow has `question_judgment`'s model-graded rubric; the timeline work-item flow — `temporal_timeline._group_claims`, `compose_question`, and `identity_resolution`'s candidate builders — never applied a quality bar of its own. A claim joined or minted a node on subject-plus-event-kind alone (`_group_claims`, lines 1660–1678); `compose_question`'s fallback row read a gerund phrase as a noun; and `identity_resolution.roster_index` indexed every roster row — alias, collective, and owner alike — into the same given-name census a bare mention is checked against, so an alias row and its own canonical target both rode as separate candidates and a collective's alias could make the owner's own first name read as ambiguous. THE RULE: a card is asked only when a person could answer it, in the smallest right places, one definition each. A claim whose only label is a pronoun or a placeholder (`landmarks_interaction.EMPTY_SUBJECT_LABELS` — the existing `PLACEHOLDER_LABELS` plus a new `PRONOUN_LABELS`, whole-body casefold) AND whose `confidence` is exactly `0.0` mints no node and no work item at all (`temporal_timeline._claim_is_empty`, read from `_group_claims`) — the claim stays in the substrate, unminted, and a participation entry's own identity claim is exempt by design. BOTH signals, never either alone: `confidence: 0.0` is `temporal_claims.unit_score`'s own default for “never stated”, not a marker reserved for “found nothing worth trusting”, and an OR here refused a node to ordinary claims across the suite that simply never set it — `tests/test_eras_e3.py`'s own graduation claim among them, SEEN failing this way first before the gate was narrowed to AND. `PRONOUN_LABELS` is third-person/indefinite only — \"they\", \"he\", \"she\", \"it\", \"we\", \"someone\", \"this\", \"that\", … — and deliberately excludes \"i\"/\"me\"/\"self\", the ordinary way a raw claim's `subject_mention` names the OWNER. A bare gerund/participle `{what}` reads `\"When was {what}?\"` (`temporal_timeline._is_gerund_phrase`) rather than the ungrammatical `\"When did {what} happen?\"`; a real `-ing` event noun (\"the wedding\", \"the meeting\") is excluded by name and untouched. `identity_resolution.roster_index` drops a `maps_to_focus` row entirely — curation's own statement that the spelling is not a second identity, every spelling it carries already expected as an alias on the row it maps to — and excludes a collective/role row (a local, deliberately-duplicated copy of `entity_roster.ROLE_WORDS`; `identity_resolution.py` may never import `entity_roster` — its own purity contract, \"safe to vendor into the worker and the sandboxed prompt seam\") from the given-name census, though its own exact spelling still resolves. `identity_resolution.identity_work_item` never offers the OWNER as a candidate for the owner's own given name (`owner_refs`, from `identity_resolution.owner_name_refs` over `temporal_publication.owner_identity_inputs`'s own names — deliberately NOT `timeline-rules:9`, which is the opposite direction: an unknown name is not the owner, not the owner excluded from a name that IS his own), and fewer than two real candidates after that mints nothing. `conversation_lints._QUESTION_BARE_PRONOUN_RE` widens from `i|me|the subject|self` to include `landmarks_interaction.PRONOUN_LABELS` as a composed-text backstop, keeping the original four spellings unchanged (a rewrite that fails and leaves \"self\" in the SENTENCE is still a real defect) and never adding \"you\", the correct composed subject of every owner-directed question this fold writes. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` takes `timeline-rules:13`: the same claims now calculate to a different node set and a different work-item set for claims nobody edited, which is what moves that number. VERIFIED on a read-only rig cloned from the owner's own vault at `5bc68bd9`, v342 framework vs this v343 worktree overlaid on the identical clone: generation 151→152, nodes 1189→1188, open work items 45→40 (`identity_uncertain` 3→1, `precision_gap` 35→32, `contradiction` 7→7 unchanged). The `they` node and its card gone (the one node present before and absent after); no “When did Harvey arriving happen?” anywhere in the projection, replaced by “When was Harvey arriving?”; the James cards reduced from two (one of them a bare duplicate) to one, correctly offering “Anthon James Taylor or James Everett Taylor”; the Dave/Kids card gone. BONUS: two previously-unplaced nodes (`node:a23d6b65e9ad5f670d33a5ec`, `node:e8bcb1c4a1dd3acc981c54d5`) had carried the raw, unresolved mention “Dave” as their subject — collateral damage of the same Kids-collision bug — and now resolve to the owner and pick up real age-derived placements, closing their precision-gap cards as a side effect of the identity fix rather than a loss. Zero of the 1188 nodes common to both runs that were ALREADY placed changed `best_temporal_value`; zero unexpected new nodes; the only node removed is the `they` node. GUARDS: `tests/test_v343_card_quality_gate.py` (41 tests) — each of the four owner cards reproduced as a fixture (a confidence-zero pronoun claim and its `self`/`you` false-positive guards, a gerund-phrase composition and its `-ing`-noun exceptions, the James alias-row collision, the Dave/Kids collective collision, the owner-exclusion mechanism tested independently of the collective-census exclusion), the `entity_roster.ROLE_WORDS` duplicate pinned against drift, and the rule version pinned. `tests/goldens/cert_10_place_containment.json` and the six other test files that pinned `timeline-rules:12` move to `:13` (`test_axis_membership.py`, `test_eras_e1.py`, `test_eras_e2.py`, `test_event_identity_i0_fold.py`, `test_event_identity_i1_fold.py`, `test_place_containment.py`). Full suite: 6392 tests, one pre-existing macOS-only failure unchanged from main (`test_file_answer_outer_queue_leaves_no_plaintext_temp`), zero other regressions against a from-scratch `origin/main` baseline run. Docs: `docs/handbook/eras.md`'s rule-version row and parity annotation move to `timeline-rules:13`; `docs/handbook/interactions/timeline.md`'s question-writer rules gain a fifth bullet; `docs/handbook/entities.md` notes the roster's `maps_to_focus` and collective/role fields now also gate identity resolution, not only page eligibility.",
